#problem[Let $sigma $ be a k-cycle. Prove that $sigma^i $ is also a k-cycle if and only if $i $ is relatively prime to $k$ ][

Note that $sigma^i (a_j) = a_(j+i mod k)$ where if $j+i>k$ then we mod $j+i$ by $k$

This is because, mod $k$ we have  $ sigma^i (a_j) = sigma^(i-1) sigma(a_j) = sigma^(i-1) (a_(j+1)) = sigma^(i-2) sigma(a_(j+1)) = sigma^(i-2) (a_(j+2))=dots=sigma^(i-i) sigma(a_(j+i-1)) = a_(j+i) $ 

Using this fact, we that continuous applications of $sigma^i$ to $a_k$ will yield $ a_(k) arrow.bar a_(k+i) arrow.bar a_(k+2i) arrow.bar dots arrow.bar a_(k+(k-1)i) arrow.bar a_k $

Since each $k + x i, k + y i$ are distinct then we have $k+x i equiv.not k + y i arrow.double i(x-y) equiv.not 0$ $mod k$   

Next we need to show that $i(x-y) equiv.not 0 mod k arrow.double.l.r (i,k)=1$

$arrow.double.r$ 

Suppose $(i,k)=d > 1$ then let $i=i' d, k = k' d$

Then $i' d (x-y) equiv.not 0 mod k'd arrow.double k' d divides.not i' d (x-y) arrow.double k' divides.not i' (x-y)$ 

$arrow.double.l $ is true since $x-y eq.not 0$ and $i$ cannot be a factor of $k$ by coprime definition

$qed$ 

Sources: \
(1) https://www.uvm.edu/~mrombach/251-H2-sols.pdf \
(2) https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/659222/let-sigma-be-the-m-cycle-1-2-ldots-m-prove-that-sigma-i-is-also-a

