#problem[Prove the the order of an element in $S_n $ equals the least common multiple of the lengths of the cycle in the cycle decomposition][

Note that a $k $ cycle will have order $k $ 

Therefore, for some $k $ cycle $sigma $ we have $sigma^(k) = 1 = sigma^(n k)$ for some $n in ZZ$ \

If some $sigma in S_n$ has (disjoint) cycle decomposition such that $sigma = tau_1 dots tau_m$ where $tau_i in S_n, i in {1,dots,m}$  then $ sigma^r = (tau_1 dots tau_m)^r = tau_1^r dots tau_m^r = 1 arrow.double.l.r tau_1^r = 1, dots, tau_m^r = 1 $

Where the second equality holds due to $tau_1, dots, tau_m$ being disjoint cycles, recall disjoint cycles commute with eachother 

Therefore the order of $sigma$ denoted $r$ must be a multiple of each of $|tau_1|, dots, |tau_m|$ 

Furthermore, this multiple must be as small as possible, or in other words $r$ is the least common multiple of the orders, or in other words the least common multiple of the lengths of the cycle

$qed$  

]
#problem[Prove that every $sigma in S_n $ can be written as a product of adjacent elements, that is, transpositions of the form $(i, i+1)$. This was one of the exercises I had given in class  ][

  From class, we know that every $sigma in S_n$ can be written as a product of disjoint cycles \

  Also, we know that any cycle $cyc(a_1, a_2, dots, a_k)=(a_1 a_2)(a_2 a_3) dots (a_(k-1) a_k)$ where $a_1 dots a_k$ are distinct  \

  Any transposition $cyc(a,b)$ where $a < b$ can be written as (i) $ cyc(a b) = cyc(a, a + 1)· · ·cyc(b - 2, b - 1)cyc(b - 1, b)cyc(b - 2, b - 1)· · ·cyc(a, a + 1) $  \

  In the case of $cyc(a, b)$ where $a > b$ consider (constructed by the above), $ cyc(b, a) = (b, b + 1)· · ·cyc(a-2,a-1)cyc(a-1,a)cyc(a-2,a-1)· · ·cyc(b, b+1) $  

  And $ cyc(b, a)^(-1) = cyc(b, b+1)· · ·cyc(a-2, a-1)cyc(a-1,a)cyc(a-2,a-1)· · ·cyc(b,b+1) = cyc(a,b) $ 

  Since $(sigma_1 dots sigma_n)^(-1) = sigma_n^(-1) dots sigma_1^(-1)$ and a two cycle is its own inverse

  $qed$

  example: 

  (1 2)(2 3)(1 2)  = (1 3) \

  (3 1) = (1 2) (2 3) ( 1 2)

  references: \
  (i) https://www.albany.edu/~am815139/327.2015.ReviewExam2Sol.pdf \
