#problem[Let $G$ be an Abelian group. Prove that the set ${g in G : |g| < infty}$ is a subgroup of $G $. This is called the torsion subfroup of $G$. Give an example where this set is not a subgroup when $G $ is non-abelian  ][

Denote $H := {g in G:|g|<infty}$ 

We can try to show that $H eq.not emptyset$ and that $forall x,y in H$ that $x y^(-1) in H$ \

First, note that $1 in G$ has order 1 so that $H eq.not emptyset$

Also, if $x in H, y in H$ let their orders be $|x|=n, |y|=m arrow.double |y^(-1)|=m$ then $(x y^(-1))^(n m) = x^(n m) (y^(-1))^(n m) = 1^m 1^n = 1$ and therefore $x y^(-1)$ has order $n m$ and is in $H$   

We can recall that since $G$ is abelian and $x,y in G$ that $(x y)^n = x^n y^n$ for all $n in ZZ$ and that an element and its inverse have the same order (homework 2) $qed$

Consider the non-abelian group $G L_2 (QQ) = {A_(2 times 2)(QQ):det A eq.not 0}$ \

Note that $mat(0,-1;1,0)$ has order 4 and $mat(0,1;-1,-1)$ has order 3, but $mat(0,-1;1,0) mat(0,1;-1,-1)=mat(1,1;0,1)$ which has infinite order (since $mat(1,1;0,1)^n = mat(1,n;0,1)$ ), and therefore the set ${g in GL_2(QQ):|g|<infty}$ is not closed, and therefore not a subgroup    

References:

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_linear_group

]
#problem[

1. Prove that if $H $ and $K $ are subgroups of a group $G$, then so is their intersection $H sect K$  

2. Prove that the intersection of an arbitrary non-empty collection of subgroups of a group $G $ is again a subgroup of $G$ (do not assume the collection is countable) 

][

1. Firstly, $1 in G arrow.double 1 in H and 1 in K arrow.double 1 in H sect K$

Secondly, $a,b in H sect K arrow.double a in H and a in K and b in H and b in K arrow.double a b in H and a b in K arrow.double a b in H sect K$

Thirdly, $a in H sect K arrow.double a in H and a in K arrow.double a^(-1) in H and a^(-1) in K arrow.double a^(-1) in H sect K$

Where each part is justified by properties of subgroups $qed$ 

2. Let $H_1 dots H_2 dots$ be a collection of subgroups of $G$, then since each pair of $H_i$ is a subgroup from part a) we know that their intersection $((H_1 sect H_2) sect H_3)sect dots$ will also be a subgroup, similar to generalized associative law. $qed$ 

]
#problem[Let $H_1 lt.eq H_2 lt.eq dots$ be an asecnding chain of subgroups of a group $G$. Prove that $union_(i=0)^infty H_i$ is a subgroup of $G$     ][

We can combine the following facts:

1. if $A subset.eq B$ and $B subset.eq C$ then $A union B subset.eq C$

2. $H_1 union H_2$ is a subgroup iff $H_1 subset.eq H_2$ or $H_2 subset.eq H_1$   

For fact a note that $x in A union B arrow.double x in A or x in B$. If $x in B arrow.double x in C$ and if $x in A arrow.double x in B arrow.double x in C$ by the initial assumption \

For fact b we can use a weaker version which is that $H_1 subset.eq H_2 arrow.double H_1 union H_2$ is a subgroup

To show this, consider that (by definition)$H_1 subset.eq H_2 arrow.double H_1 union H_2 = H_2$ and that $H_2$ is a subgroup by assumption  

Next, $H_1 union H_2 subset.eq H_3 arrow.double H_1 union H_2 union H_3 = H_3$ is a subgroup and we can keep applying fact b then fact a and conclude that $union_(i=0)^infty H_i$ is a subgroup of $G$

$qed$ 

references:\
https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/3634918/for-two-subgroups-h-1-h-2-how-can-i-show-h-1-cup-h-2-is-subgroup-iff

]
